### FINDING 14 — Rate Limit Bypass via `X-Forwarded-For` Spoofing *(FIXED 16 Sep 2026)*

**Severity: HIGH** — recorded here because it is the most serious issue found in this pass.

**Files:** `app/api/auth/login/route.ts`, `app/api/auth/signup/route.ts`

The rate limiter keyed on the **first** entry of `X-Forwarded-For`:

```ts
const ip = req.headers.get('x-forwarded-for')?.split(',')[0]?.trim() ?? 'unknown';
```

That entry is supplied by the client. The proxy appends the observed IP *after* any
value the caller sent, so the first element is attacker-controlled.

**Confirmed against production**, not inferred: a login request carrying
`X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.77` (a reserved TEST-NET address that cannot be a real
client) produced the rate-limit row `login:203.0.113.77`.

**Risk:** the 5-attempts-per-15-minutes login throttle — the only defence against
password brute force — was bypassable by rotating one header. An attacker could make
unlimited credential guesses against any known email address. Signup abuse and
unbounded `rate_limits` growth followed from the same flaw.

**Fix:** `app/lib/clientIp.ts` resolves the caller from `x-nf-client-connection-ip`,
which Netlify overwrites on every request. A forged value was sent and confirmed to
lose to the real IP. `X-Forwarded-For` is now only a fallback, and reads the **last**
hop rather than the first.

**Verified after deploy:** the same spoofed request no longer creates a
`login:203.0.113.99` row; the limiter keys on the genuine client IP.

